export type InViewOptions = { threshold?: number; rootMargin?: string; once?: boolean }
export function useInView<T extends Element>(options?: InViewOptions): { ref: React.RefCallback<T>; inView: boolean }

- Defaults: threshold 0, rootMargin '0px', once true.
- When IntersectionObserver is missing, the lazy initial state is true.
- setState happens only in the observer callback.
- The observer is cleaned up on unmount.

export type SegmentedOption<V extends string> = { value: V; label: string }
export type SegmentedControlProps<V extends string> = { legend: string; value: V; options: ReadonlyArray<SegmentedOption<V>>; onChange: (value: V) => void; disabled?: boolean; hideLegend?: boolean; fullWidth?: boolean }
export function SegmentedControl<V extends string>(props: SegmentedControlProps<V>): ReactElement

- Renders fieldset.lp-seg with a legend (.lp-seg-legend, or .lp-seg-legend-hidden when hideLegend is set).
- Then .lp-seg-options (plus .lp-seg-full when fullWidth is set), holding labels that each wrap input.lp-seg-input[type=radio] and span.lp-seg-pill.
- The radio name comes from useId.

export function useInPageLink(): (event: React.MouseEvent<HTMLAnchorElement>) => void

- Resolves the '#id' href.
- If the target exists and supports scrollIntoView, it:
  - calls preventDefault;
  - calls scrollIntoView({ behavior: reduced ? 'auto' : 'smooth', block: 'start' });
  - calls history.pushState(null, '', '#' + id);
  - focuses the target with preventScroll.
- Otherwise it leaves native behaviour alone.
- It never sets scroll-behavior on html.

Tests heroFrame:
- stats at 0, 3000, 4500, 6000 and 60000 ms;
- running is never negative;
- approvalArrived persists after 60000;
- three trace lines at 3000 and none at 9700.

Tests:
- the reducer paths: approve (ends completed), reject, and let-expire (ends expired);
- employee and viewer cannot APPROVE or REJECT;
- LET_EXPIRE works for any decider;
- the reduced initial state is parked.

With jsdom (reduced motion is on because matchMedia is missing):
- the demo renders parked, showing 'Exactly what will be sent';
- clicking Approve shows the status 'The run resumed on the same model and completed.';
- choosing Employee hides Approve.

No '!' in JSX text.

Tests:
- wordCount matches the plain text;
- sliceWords never splits a citation marker;
- passage 1 is page 5 and passage 2 is page 4.

export function RoleSwitcher(): ReactElement

Renders LandingSection#roles with SectionHead, then .lp-roles containing:
- the tablist (5 tabs, roving tabindex, arrow keys, Home and End, automatic activation, meters);
- the tabpanel: header with the count from useCountUp, and the 'Compare with' native select;
- the 'In plain words' checklist, the console areas, and the permission map with compare mode;
- the footer link and hedge;
- a visually-hidden polite announcer.

Default role: manager. The spec is in 9.3.

Tests:
- role sizes are 46, 45, 25, 12 and 8;
- ALL_CODES has 46 unique codes and equals the union of the groups;
- employee is a subset of manager;
- compareRoles(manager, employee) gives 13 added and 0 removed;
- console-area counts are 11, 11, 10, 8 and 7.

export function AuditChainDemo(): ReactElement

Renders DemoFrame (area audit, index 03) containing:
- ol.lp-audit-list of 4 inset entries with 'seq NNNN' labels, the text (del and ins while altered), and the prev and hash chips;
- connectors that turn danger with the link-broken Icon on a mismatch;
- the 'Alter entry 2' aria-pressed button;
- the computed status and the tampered caveat.

Everything is computed during render with useMemo. There are no effects. The spec is in 9.2.

Tests:
- the untampered chain verifies;
- tamper marks entry 2 altered and entry 3 prevMismatch only;
- fnv1a32 is deterministic and always 8 characters long.

export function PlatformBand(): ReactElement

Renders LandingSection#platform with a band SectionHead ('Under the hood', 'What is actually running'), then .lp-platform.lp-frost containing:
- a 5-cell hairline stats grid with useCountUp values (aria-hidden, plus visually-hidden final values);
- a 'Model providers' row of neutral Tags;
- a 'Tool servers' row of mono chips.

The spec is in 9.4.

Keep every existing test unchanged.

Add describe('landing styles'), which reads every .css file in src/styles/landing/ and checks:
- (a) index.css imports every other file;
- (b) the border rule applies to all landing files, with at least 6 matches;
- (c) the radius-token regex from spec 10;
- (d) no hex;
- (e) keyframes are prefixed lp-.

Renders <Landing /> inside RouterProvider and checks:
- globalThis.fetch is spied and never called;
- axe-core reports no violations, with color-contrast disabled;
- reduced end states are present: 'Exactly what will be sent', an 'Answered' tag, 'Project_Proposal.pdf', and the manager tab aria-selected;
- exactly one h1.

This file lives outside src/routes/ on purpose, because the eyebrow test scans every .tsx file under src/routes/.
